2026-08-27T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-1152) 'The segmenter clusters near 1.0 as predicted. The matting variants run higher, and the high-resolution variant is the most consistent.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-1152) '**This ratio ranks model families and must not be read per image.** The 4.01 is the highest number in the table and is not transparency: it is a soft uncertainty blob. The metric conflates partial alpha with an unsure model.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-1152) 'Cost, and the dtype that decides it
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-1152) 'HR-matting runs at 2048px, 5.7 s per image measured over 27 images on M2 Pro MPS, against ~1.2 s for the 1024px variants. Weights are 220M parameters.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-1152) '**Float16, not bfloat16.** BiRefNet's ASPP uses deformable convolutions, and MPS ships `deformable_im2col_half` but not `deformable_im2col_bfloat`, so bf16 fails inside the forward pass while fp16 is fine. Reading that bf16-specific error as "no half precision available" forced fp32, and fp32 at 2048x2048 exhausts 32 GB of unified memory: one image per five minutes with the machine paging, which looked like the model being infeasible on this hardware. The model is feasible: the bf16 error was read as a missing precision. The dtype is not about the weights, which are under a gigabyte either way; it is about activations, where a single feature map at that resolution runs to gigabytes.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-1152) 'Settled by ground truth
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-1152) 'The alphamatting.com training set has true alpha, so the ordering does not need a judge. On the 16 images all three backends completed (lower is better):
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-1152) '| backend | SAD | Gradient | Connectivity |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-1152) '|---|---:|---:|---:|
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-1152) '| birefnet | 7.09 | 0.076 | 6.00 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-1152) '| birefnet-matting | 4.75 | 0.032 | 3.80 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-1152) '| birefnet-hr-matting | **4.42** | **0.026** | **3.16** |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-1152) '`hr-matting < matting < segmenter` on every metric, none dissenting. HR-matting separately completed all 27 images alone: SAD 4.48, MSE 1.370, gradient 0.04, connectivity 3.97, consistent with the subset.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-1152) 'This also corrects the `soft_per_perimeter` proxy, which had ranked HR-matting first for the right answer by an unreliable route, and RFD66606-1153's judged ordering, which had ranked the segmenter best on gradient, inverted, since ground truth puts it roughly 3x worse.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1152  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-27T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-1152) 'The problem
